2.0.1 (unreleased)
----------
* Fix infinite loop when using --blocksize larger than 1GB
* Fix output artefacts
* Fix build on Apple Silicon
* Show warnings by default
* Require Rust 1.83+ for building from source
* Add --deterministic and --verify to check whether a device really stores the data written to it
* Wait at the end of a wipe until all data is on the device, and fail if it couldn't be written
* On Linux, --verify reads the data from the device itself and not from the cache of the system
* On Linux, write to the device itself and not into the cache of the system, which is faster, shows the real progress and reports a failed write at once
* Fail if a device takes no more data before its end is reached
* On macOS, still wait until the system wrote all data if the drive can't be told to write out its cache
* Write blocks to the device from the memory they were generated in instead of copying them first, which makes wipes of very fast SSDs faster. For this, --blocksize is rounded up to a multiple of 4096 bytes
* --verify reads the device in a thread of its own and compares the data without copying it first, which makes it more than twice as fast on fast SSDs
* --verify shows how much data differs and where that begins as soon as it finds it, and not only at the end

2.0.0
----------
Reimplement in Rust

